Programs this compiler answers differently from clang, kept as programs.

Every file here is a known wrong answer, so nothing in this directory gates
anything: `tests/open.sh` counts and always exits 0. That is the point of the
directory rather than a weakness of it. A defect put in tests/cases turns the
three boxes red until somebody mends it, and a defect written down only in
prose is one `tools/exclusions` cannot see and no run will ever mention - which
is how the tail of docs/audit-2026-09-06.html came to sit unworked underneath
four green suites for three days. This is the third place.

Each program carries a `<name>.clang` holding the oracle's answer: `REFUSE` on
a line of its own, or `ACCEPT` followed by the exact stdout, recorded with

    clang++ -x c++ -std=c++11 -pedantic-errors

A case is closed when cxx1 agrees with that file, and open.sh says so by name.
**A closed case does not belong here.** Move it to tests/cases with its
.expected or .error, where the suites will hold it from then on, and delete it
from this directory - a register that keeps its fixed entries stops being a
list of what is wrong.

Two rules about what may be added:

  - it must be a shape somebody writes. A defect nobody can reach is a note for
    docs/CONFORMANCE.md, not a program here.
  - where the defect is a leak, a double destroy, or a destructor on a slot
    nothing built, count objects with ../cases/lifetime.h and print the ledger.
    C++11 permits elision, so a constructor trace differs legitimately between
    two conforming compilers and only the balance says anything. See
    catch-return-end-catch.cpp, where cxx1 prints built=2 gone=1 live=1 and
    clang built=1 gone=1 live=0: the same program, one leaked exception object,
    and identical output above the ledger line.

Thirteen are here now, and the number is a coincidence rather than a
standstill: two of the original fourteen - capture-default-this and
polymorphic-class-template-zts - were mended the same day they were written
down and moved to tests/cases with their .expected, which is the movement the
rule above asks for; and mem-init-template-id-base arrived, found while
measuring the second of those. A register that only ever grows is a list of
complaints, and one that only ever shrinks is finished.

The original fourteen were re-measured on 2026-09-09 at 48f28a3, one program each,
from the tail of docs/audit-2026-09-06.html that the five roots did not cover -
the fourteen refusals of ordinary code, the twenty-seven over-acceptances and
the _ZTS assembler failure, none of which went with the roots. That report says
its own programs live in the reviewers' scratch directories; these are in the
repository, which is the difference between a finding and a test.
